# Laravel Eloquent pagination control page number with route

In a typical Laravel application, you often start with the standard `Articles::paginate(10)` call. This retrieves the first ten records for the initial page load. However, when implementing dynamic routing like `mypage.com/articles/2`, developers frequently encounter an issue: the default query always fetches page one, ignoring the URL parameter entirely. The goal is to dynamically pass a specific page number from the route into the Eloquent query builder.

The most direct approach involves capturing the URL segment and injecting it directly into the `paginate` method. You can define your route as `Route::get('articles/{page}', 'Controller@getArticles')`. In the controller, you simply pass the `$page` variable to the pagination helper.

```php
public function getArticles($page)
{
    $perPage = 10;

    // Ensure a default of 1 if an empty string is passed
    $page = max(1, intval($page));

    return Articles::paginate($perPage, ['*'], 'page', $page);
}
```

This implementation works because the `paginate` method accepts a third argument to name the query parameter and a fourth argument to set its value. By passing `$page` as the final argument, you override the default behavior of always fetching the first page.

A common pitfall with this approach is handling URL parameters that are empty or invalid. If a user visits `mypage.com/articles/`, the route might pass an empty string or `null`. Passing `null` to `paginate` can cause unexpected behavior or errors depending on the Laravel version and underlying configuration. To prevent this, you should normalize the input immediately. The code snippet above uses `max(1, intval($page))` to ensure the page number is always at least 1. If a user attempts to access `mypage.com/articles/0`, they will still receive the first page rather than an empty result set or an error.

While you might be tempted to use a static method like `Articles::pageNumber($page_number)->paginate($perPage)`, such a helper does not exist in the standard Eloquent documentation. The `paginate` method is designed to work within the query builder chain, but setting the current page number requires passing it as an argument rather than calling a separate setter function on the paginator instance. Attempting to manually manipulate the internal state of the paginator object returned by `paginate()` is generally discouraged, as it can lead to inconsistencies with Laravel's built-in pagination views and links.

One subtle detail involves how Eloquent generates the "previous" and "next" page links. The standard `links()` method automatically calculates these based on the current page number you provide. If you pass a page number that is mathematically impossible (e.g., requesting page 100 when there are only 5 pages), Laravel will gracefully handle the request by showing the last available page and adjusting the links accordingly. This prevents broken links in your navigation.

If you need more granular control over which columns are retrieved per page to optimize performance, you can pass an array of fields as the second argument: `Articles::paginate($perPage, ['id', 'title'], 'page', $page)`. This ensures that even if the page parameter is manipulated via the route, the query remains efficient and returns only the necessary data.

For further details on advanced pagination strategies or customizing the paginator in Laravel, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) provide additional context on optimizing database queries for high-traffic applications.
